00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.719
Leah: So, what is the Waterfall approach?

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:07.930
Chris: Waterfall project management
emphasises a linear approach from the

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.590
beginning to the end of a project.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:14.520
It flows like a waterfall through all
phases of a project with each phase

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.729
wrapping up before the next phase begins.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.120
It is often used by engineers and
is front loaded relying on careful

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.200
planning, detailed documentation,
and consecutive execution.

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:29.740
It follows a chronological
process and works on fixed dates,

00:00:29.809 --> 00:00:31.329
requirements, and outcomes.

00:00:32.849 --> 00:00:36.129
Leah: In Waterfall Project
Management there are typically four

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:39.950
main roles across the team Let’s
have a look at what these are.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.440
The Project Manager, who is the
Leader of the team and responsible

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.060
for quality and deadlines.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.930
They are responsible for managing
the project and subdividing the

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.619
tasks to the other team members.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.170
Chris: A Business Analyst, whose role
involves research and helping the team

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.990
to understand the requirements to make
the product competitive in the market.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.700
Developers who build the product
and create and write the code.

00:01:04.469 --> 00:01:07.929
Testers who test the final
product making sure it functions

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.699
correctly and there are no bugs.

00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:13.530
Leah: There are three main
principles with the Waterfall model

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.324
Chris: Low customer involvement is
connected to strong documentation

00:01:16.774 --> 00:01:19.514
as this is one of the reasons that
they don’t need to be involved

00:01:19.554 --> 00:01:21.214
in every stage of the project.

00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:25.334
For most projects there are only two
meetings with the client, initially

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:28.994
before the project starts which is where
the learning and documentation of the

00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:33.504
client’s requirements takes place, and
then a second meeting which is after the

00:01:33.504 --> 00:01:35.574
final product is ready to be deployed.

00:01:35.624 --> 00:01:39.354
Leah: Strong documentation is created
at every stage of the project.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:44.364
This includes initial documents created
in agreement with the customer which

00:01:44.364 --> 00:01:48.144
would include the requirements of the
client for the final software product.

00:01:48.264 --> 00:01:51.704
Chris: Sequential structure is the
main principle of the Waterfall model,

00:01:52.254 --> 00:01:55.064
and most projects have 5 to 7 stages.

00:01:55.954 --> 00:01:58.394
Leah: Let’s have a look at
these stages in more detail.

00:01:58.804 --> 00:01:59.454
Stage 1.

00:01:59.714 --> 00:02:01.364
Analysis and requirements

00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:04.564
Chris: This is the stage where
all the customer requirements are

00:02:04.574 --> 00:02:08.914
gathered by the team in the form of
a document which would include costs,

00:02:09.074 --> 00:02:13.404
assumptions, risks, dependencies,
measurements, and a timeline.

00:02:14.174 --> 00:02:18.044
This meeting would be very thorough to
ensure that there wouldn’t be a need

00:02:18.044 --> 00:02:21.484
for further communication with the
client until the end of the project.

00:02:22.004 --> 00:02:22.814
Leah: Stage 2.

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:23.484
Design

00:02:23.484 --> 00:02:26.080
This is the stage where the
team would design a technical

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.580
solution for the project.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.460
It would include logical and
physical design and would involve

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.380
the team and the Project Manager.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:34.650
Chris: Stage 3.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:35.850
Implementation

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.560
the team would start to create the
actual code based on the requirements

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.690
and specification that has been
identified in the previous stages.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:44.880
Leah: Stage 4.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.550
Testing and verification

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.380
Testing is done at this stage by
the QA team to make sure that there

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:53.980
are no errors and that all the
requirements have been completed.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.190
User testing may also
be done at this stage.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.380
Chris: Stage 5.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.030
Deployment and release

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.570
Once the client is happy with the
product and it has been reviewed and

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.050
signed off, the product can be deployed.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:07.490
Leah: Stage 6.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.740
Maintenance

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:13.256
This stage would be to ensure if
there are any errors or bugs, updates,

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:17.766
and or new features that are needed
once the product has been released.

